{"type":"assistant","message":{"model":"claude-sonnet-5-5","id":"msg_011CfwZ6CLa5tUdDx7rHMBGD","type":"message","role":"assistant","content":[{"type":"text","text":"The dashboard and the alert rule both exist, and the rule is enabled. I haven't opened the dashboard to check how the panels render. The rule's status is still \"pending\", so it hasn't run yet.\n\n**What the data holds:** Web server access logs, with the timestamp in `timestamp`. The fields I used are `response` (the HTTP status code, stored as a string), `bytes`, `extension`, `clientip`, `geo.src` and `geo.dest`, `machine.os`, `request` and `url`.\n\n**Dashboard:** \"EFF-ab8863cf Web logs overview\" has four panels and defaults to the last 7 days. It's at http://localhost:15601/app/dashboards#/view/eff-ab8863cf-web-logs-overview-41ab7bdbe18a.\n- A metric panel with the total request count.\n- A line chart of requests over time.\n- A pie chart of requests by response code.\n- A bar chart of average bytes by file extension.\n\n**Alert rule:** \"EFF-ab8863cf 5xx spike\" (id `214ee29f-7cf1-4db1-b425-a88a32a3296f`) is an Elasticsearch query rule that runs every minute. It fires when more than 10 documents in the last 5 minutes have a response code starting with \"5\". It reads the `kibana_sample_data_logs` index directly. No notification action is attached, so it will register alerts in Kibana but won't send them anywhere.\n\nThe sample logs are timestamped around when the data was installed, so with no fresh traffic the rule may never fire."}],"container":null,"stop_reason":null,"stop_sequence":null,"stop_details":null,"usage":{"input_tokens":2,"cache_creation_input_tokens":1287,"cache_read_input_tokens":18870,"cache_creation":{"ephemeral_5m_input_tokens":0,"ephemeral_1h_input_tokens":1287},"output_tokens":511,"service_tier":"standard","inference_geo":"global"},"input_transformations":[],"diagnostics":null,"context_management":null},"parent_tool_use_id":null,"session_id":"a4eb1cb7-866c-5bbf-8d81-a8d5acd83b